From 4acc4e4ea533b4dc3b33b56530fe4dc27cc3cade Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:01:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add transactional code editing: edit -> test -> keep/rollback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the last open item from Phase 01's known limitations list:
"transactional code editing (edit -> test -> keep/rollback)".

Previously a code change was write_file + a separate run_command the
model had to remember to issue, with nothing undoing a bad edit if
the follow-up check failed. transactions.py makes edit+verify+rollback
one atomic runtime operation instead of a multi-step pattern the model
has to get right on its own:

- transactions.apply_change_with_test(workspace, filename, content,
  test_command, backend): snapshots the file's current content (or
  notes it didn't exist), writes the new content, runs test_command
  through the real sandbox backend, and on a nonzero exit restores the
  file to exactly its prior content (or deletes it, if this call
  created it).

- forge.py: new apply_change_with_test tool, registered in TOOLS with
  risk="write", sandboxed=True. record_evidence() files its outcome
  into a new "transactional edits" bucket and, when the test command
  is a real test runner, into the existing test execution / failed
  test execution buckets — keyed on whether the change was actually
  kept, not just attempted.

- build_evidence_summary() reports kept vs rolled-back edits
  explicitly, and the grounding instructions now tell the model never
  to describe a rolled-back edit as if it survived.

- System prompt documents the new tool and a JSON example, with an
  explicit instruction not to follow it with a redundant verification
  call (the tool loop will cache that into the model's own history,
  but telling it up front avoids wasting a step).

test_transactions.py (6 tests): keeps on pass, rolls back an existing
file on fail, deletes a newly-created file on fail, rejects workspace
escape, reports real exit code/output, and a real failing unittest
run actually triggers the rollback end-to-end (no mocked command).

test_forge.py: two new end-to-end tests driving the full run_task()
loop — one where the model's edit passes and is kept, one where it
breaks the real test suite and is proven to be rolled back to the
exact original file content, with the grounded summary required to
say "ROLLED BACK" rather than imply the change stuck.

46/46 tests pass (38 prior + 8 new). This closes out Phase 01
("Reliable core") entirely — every item in the known-limitations list
from the vision doc is now addressed.

